WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.172
After authentication, every sensitive
operation still requires authorization.

2
00:00:05.252 --> 00:00:08.922
The server evaluates the trusted subject,
the requested action,

3
00:00:09.002 --> 00:00:11.471
the target resource, and relevant context.

4
00:00:11.551 --> 00:00:15.889
Route-level permissions may establish that
a role can use an administrative

5
00:00:15.969 --> 00:00:20.507
capability, but object-level rules still
determine which particular record is

6
00:00:20.587 --> 00:00:21.321
accessible.

7
00:00:21.401 --> 00:00:25.205
Ownership, tenant membership, delegated
access, workflow state,

8
00:00:25.285 --> 00:00:28.555
and resource classification can all affect
the decision.

9
00:00:28.635 --> 00:00:31.839
A UUID or opaque URL may make identifiers
harder to guess;

10
00:00:31.919 --> 00:00:33.520
it never proves permission.

11
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.271
Whenever possible, carry access
constraints into the data path.

12
00:00:37.351 --> 00:00:42.022
A lookup by document ID alone can retrieve
another tenant's record before policy is

13
00:00:42.102 --> 00:00:42.837
considered.

14
00:00:42.917 --> 00:00:47.455
A scoped query that includes both the
requested ID and the trusted tenant ID makes

15
00:00:47.535 --> 00:00:50.137
cross-tenant data ineligible for
retrieval.

16
00:00:50.217 --> 00:00:54.155
Similar constraints can cover owner IDs,
organization memberships,

17
00:00:54.235 --> 00:00:57.772
soft-deletion state, or a permitted
collection of resources.

18
00:00:57.852 --> 00:01:01.723
Exact syntax varies by ORM and database,
but the principle is stable:

19
00:01:01.803 --> 00:01:03.805
derive scope from trusted context,

20
00:01:03.885 --> 00:01:06.955
not from a tenantId or ownerId submitted
by the client.

21
00:01:07.035 --> 00:01:09.571
Query scoping does not replace every
policy.

22
00:01:09.651 --> 00:01:13.655
A retrieved record may require an
additional decision based on action,

23
00:01:13.735 --> 00:01:16.604
role, delegation, sensitivity, or current
state.

24
00:01:16.684 --> 00:01:21.222
Put that logic in a named policy or
application service that receives the trusted

25
00:01:21.302 --> 00:01:24.039
AuthContext and authoritative resource
facts.

26
00:01:24.119 --> 00:01:28.657
Deny by default when required context is
absent or no rule explicitly permits the

27
00:01:28.737 --> 00:01:29.204
action.

28
00:01:29.284 --> 00:01:33.822
This is easier to review than scattered
checks whose success depends on handlers

29
00:01:33.902 --> 00:01:35.771
remembering one more condition.

30
00:01:35.851 --> 00:01:39.855
Apply authorization to the entire
operation surface: reads, updates,

31
00:01:39.935 --> 00:01:44.873
deletes, downloads, exports, nested
resources, workflow actions, and bulk requests.

32
00:01:44.953 --> 00:01:47.623
Bulk operations need each target
constrained,

33
00:01:47.703 --> 00:01:50.172
not merely permission to call the
endpoint.

34
00:01:50.252 --> 00:01:54.790
Alternate routes that reach the same
service must receive the same enforcement.

35
00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:59.608
A hidden edit button or client-side route
guard can present the correct interface to

36
00:01:59.688 --> 00:02:04.427
ordinary users, but a direct API request
reaches the server without that interface.

37
00:02:04.507 --> 00:02:09.178
The endpoint remains safe only when
server-side policy independently reaches the

38
00:02:09.258 --> 00:02:11.060
same decision on every request.
